NFS4SAF — licensing overview
==========================

This repository contains components under different licenses. It is not entirely
Apache-2.0. This overview identifies their scope; it does not replace any license
text, remove upstream notices, or relicense third-party code.

1. NFS4SAF-authored material — Apache License, Version 2.0

Except where an existing file notice says otherwise, the following original
material is provided under Apache-2.0:

- app/src/main/java/info/gdsnyder/**
- protocol/src/main/java/info/gdsnyder/**
- protocol/src/main/java/org/dcache/oncrpc4j/xdr/ByteArrayXdr.java
  (an original adapter despite its placement in an upstream namespace)
- original tests, build configuration, documentation and project artwork.

The full terms are in licenses/Apache-2.0.txt.

2. Vendored dCache NFS4J and ONCRPC4J — upstream GNU Library/Lesser GPL

The remaining source under protocol/src/main/java/org/dcache/nfs/** and
protocol/src/main/java/org/dcache/oncrpc4j/** comes from the upstream libraries
identified in THIRD_PARTY.md. Original copyright and license notices apply.
Modifications to those files remain subject to their applicable upstream terms;
they are not covered only by the license for our original application code.

Files carrying the GNU Library GPL version 2-or-later header retain those terms
(SPDX: LGPL-2.0-or-later). Some generated NFS types carry a generator provenance
header instead of an individual license grant; they are shipped as part of the
upstream LGPL package. Do not infer an Apache license from a missing LGPL header.
The exact upstream license version for those generator-only files should be
confirmed before selecting a single license expression for a public release.

Copies of LGPL-2.0, LGPL-2.1, LGPL-3.0 and GPL-3.0 are in licenses/. Their presence
alone does not mean each license applies to every file or grant a new license
choice beyond the upstream terms. File notices and upstream grants determine
which versions are available.

3. Dependencies resolved by the build

Apache Kerby and Guava retain their Apache-2.0 licenses and upstream notices.
JUnit Jupiter is a test-only dependency under EPL-2.0. Kerby's test KDC is
Apache-2.0. The optional Robolectric Android API jar is compilation-only and is
not distributed in the Android application. Transitive dependencies retain their
own licenses; this overview is not a complete APK dependency audit.

4. Distribution of the Android application

An APK includes both our application code and third-party library code.
Distributing it must satisfy the applicable licenses together; calling the APK
"Apache-2.0" alone does not discharge LGPL requirements. Preserve the required
notices and license texts and provide the library source, including modifications,
and the application/build material needed to rebuild or relink with a modified
library, as required by the applicable LGPL version. Do not impose restrictions
inconsistent with those permissions.

Publishing this complete source tree and build instructions is part of that
process, not a guarantee that every binary-distribution requirement is satisfied.
See THIRD_PARTY.md for versions, source artifact URLs and recorded modifications.
